{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"c155661f198211c4","name":"card_event_overcast","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Breakthrough/events/card_event_overcast","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"OVERCAST","text":"Forecast. All HQs get +2 defense. All players gain an extra kredit slot and draw a card.","flavorText":"Through history the weather has always been a major factor in warfare and in WWII the weather played an even more crucial role. Meteorological intelligence became very important as it affected the routing of ships and convoys and military planning, especially since much of it involved combined use of forces on the ground, air and sea. F.ex. D-Day was delayed by one day due to an unfavourable weather forecast. In the North Atlantic and Arctic Oceans the Axis and the Allies fought to gain control of weather data in what is known as the North Atlantic Weather War. The Germans set up secret weather stations in remote places in the Arctic, Jan Mayen, Spitsbergen and Greenland, and gathered weather data in trawlers disguised as fishing boats etc. However, with the cracking of Enigma in 1942, German weather data across Europe became accessible to the Allies.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.forecast"],"Name":"card_event_overcast","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_overcast_title","SourceString":"OVERCAST","LocalizedString":"OVERCAST"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_overcast_text","SourceString":"Forecast. All HQs get +2 defense. All players gain an extra kredit slot and draw a card.","LocalizedString":"Forecast. All HQs get +2 defense. All players gain an extra kredit slot and draw a card."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_overcast_history","SourceString":"Through history the weather has always been a major factor in warfare and in WWII the weather played an even more crucial role. Meteorological intelligence became very important as it affected the routing of ships and convoys and military planning, especially since much of it involved combined use of forces on the ground, air and sea. F.ex. D-Day was delayed by one day due to an unfavourable weather forecast. In the North Atlantic and Arctic Oceans the Axis and the Allies fought to gain control of weather data in what is known as the North Atlantic Weather War. The Germans set up secret weather stations in remote places in the Arctic, Jan Mayen, Spitsbergen and Greenland, and gathered weather data in trawlers disguised as fishing boats etc. However, with the cracking of Enigma in 1942, German weather data across Europe became accessible to the Allies.","LocalizedString":"Through history the weather has always been a major factor in warfare and in WWII the weather played an even more crucial role. Meteorological intelligence became very important as it affected the routing of ships and convoys and military planning, especially since much of it involved combined use of forces on the ground, air and sea. F.ex. D-Day was delayed by one day due to an unfavourable weather forecast. In the North Atlantic and Arctic Oceans the Axis and the Allies fought to gain control of weather data in what is known as the North Atlantic Weather War. The Germans set up secret weather stations in remote places in the Arctic, Jan Mayen, Spitsbergen and Greenland, and gathered weather data in trawlers disguised as fishing boats etc. However, with the cracking of Enigma in 1942, German weather data across Europe became accessible to the Allies."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Breakthrough","returnSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"spawnCardName":"card_event_sunny1_blue_sky;card_event_rain1_mist;card_event_storm1_gale","soundEnterPlay":{"Obje